          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Fruit primarily signifies the observable product of an inward spiritual condition—especially character, conduct, and works—which reveals whether its source is good or corrupt.\",\"book_summary\":\"Fruit signifies the works produced by persons or peoples, revealing their nature and furnishing grounds for judgment.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Observable conduct or works that disclose the character of their spiritual source.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Works that reveal the character of the producing person or people.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book emphasizes works and judgment, while the consensus also explicitly names character and Spirit-produced virtues, but these are standard forms of the same outward spiritual produce.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify fruit with observable results that reveal the nature of their source.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus identification almost exactly: fruit is what a person or community produces, and that production reveals the underlying tree or spiritual condition. Its statement that fruit supplies a basis for judgment develops the evidentiary function already present in the consensus rather than replacing or materially narrowing it.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The book's wording could be read more narrowly than the consensus because it says 'works' rather than expressly including inner virtues or character traits such as those in Galatians 5. However, in the cited tree-and-fruit contexts, works or conduct are the outward expression of character, so this does not establish a material difference in the core identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
